title: Writing Mathematics in English
author: BetterLaTeX
date: A two minute tour

abstract: Everything on this page was typed as ordinary English. There are no backslashes, no code(\begin{...}) blocks, and no LaTeX installation anywhere on this machine. Edit the text on the left and the page on the right redraws as you type.

# The idea

Anything inside \math(...) is read as English and set as mathematics. So typing math(sum from k = 1 to n of a^k x^k) gives display(sum from k = 1 to n of a^k x^k) and typing math(for all s in S) gives math(for all s in S), with the quantifier and the membership sign chosen for you.

Words that the converter recognizes turn brass in the editor. A word that stays grey is being treated as a plain variable, which is exactly what you want for math(x), math(y) and math(theta).

# Fractions and scripts

A single slash is a fraction, because that is what a slash almost always means on paper: math(1/2), math(n/(n+1)), math((x+1)/(y+2)). A double slash is a literal slash, for things like math(km//h) and set differences.

Use ^ for powers and _ for subscripts: math(a^k x^k), math(x_max), math(a_ij), math(e^(2 pi i theta)). A subscript takes the whole word, so math(x_max) reads as one name, while a power takes one letter, so math(a^kx^k) still means two factors.

# Operators read left to right

equation(integral from 0 to 1 of x^2 dx = 1/3)

equation(limit as n -> infinity of (1 + 1/n)^n = e)

align(
sum from k = 1 to n of k = n(n+1)/2
product from i = 1 to n of x_i = x_1 x_2 dots x_n
)

# Sets and logic

- math(for all epsilon > 0 there exists delta > 0 such that abs(x - y) < delta)
- math(A union B subset of C intersect D)
- math(x not in empty set)
- math(f: reals -> reals is defined as f(x) = x^2)
- math(n is at most 10 and n is at least 2)

# Things with structure

display(matrix [1, 2; 3, 4] vec x = vec b)

display(cases {x^2 if x >= 0; -x^2 otherwise})

display(binom(n, k) = n factorial / (k factorial (n - k) factorial))

# Ordinary document parts

theorem: There are infinitely many primes.

proof: Suppose the set of primes is finite, say math(p_1) through math(p_n). Then math(N = product from i = 1 to n of p_i + 1) is divisible by none of them, so its prime factors are new. This contradicts finiteness.

| Notation | English | Renders |
|---|---|---|
| `sum from a to b of` | a sum with limits | math(sum from a to b of f) |
| `sqrt of` | a square root | math(sqrt of x + 1) |
| `d/dx` | a derivative | math(d/dx) |
| `A transpose` | matrix transpose | math(A transpose) |

> Any construct can be switched off with a backslash, so \math(x) stays as text.

Press Cmd-E to save this as a PDF. Press Cmd-Shift-E to save the LaTeX source it generated, which compiles anywhere. The full list of words lives in `syntax.bltx` next door.
